<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montmajour Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">948</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montmajour Abbey is a former Benedictine monastery on a rocky hill, once an island in the marshes, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> in Provence, founded in 948. It became a rich and powerful abbey and a pilgrimage centre, with a Romanesque church, cloister and crypt, and a fortified keep added in 1369. Dissolved in the eighteenth century, its ruins were painted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Vincent van Gogh</a> and are now a historic monument.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montmajour-abbey.jpg" alt="Montmajour Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montmajour-VueG%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
